\title{Examples}
\meta{slug}{simulation}

\p{
  These examples cover two benchmark suites used to evaluate \code{baux}: fusion plasma control and device equilibria (TCV rampdown policy search over #{136{,}712} weights, TORAX radial transport, and FUSE equilibria for SPARC, ARC, ITER, and DIII-D), alongside the 56 analytical test functions in \code{baux.benchmarks.synthetic} for interactive surface inspection and high-throughput Python benchmarks.
}

\subtree{
  \title{Tokamak Console & Equilibria}

  \<div>[id]{tokamak-cad-simulation}{}

  \<details>{
  \<summary>{TCV results and experiment setup}
  \subtree{
    \title{TCV Rampdown: Comparing Search Configurations}

    \p{
      The \code{toka_gym} rampdown environment supplies the objective for this study. The plasma-rampdown problem and its simulation setting are described by Wang et al. [[Wan+25]](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63917-3) and Theiler et al. [[The+26]](https://doi.org/10.1088/1741-4326/ae6d12). Here, baux searches the weights of an #{11 \to 256 \to 256 \to 256 \to 8} feedback policy. Its #{136{,}712} parameters enter a rollout, and the resulting error becomes the objective value.
    }

    \p{
      We compare eight configurations over 10,000 rounds with ten paired seeds: four sharing policies for ten-region search without a surrogate, and four distance choices for one-region ENN search. The recorded labels are \code{TuRBO-zero} and \code{TuRBO-ENN}; the former uses the TuRBO region controller without the original algorithm's GP surrogate. Each round evaluates one proposed policy. The budget therefore covers 800,000 proposed-policy evaluations across the configurations and seeds.
    }

    \p{
      All four surrogate-free sharing configurations have the same recorded trajectory and finish at mean error \code{1.3112 ± 0.0157}. The ENN configurations finish between \code{1.3409 ± 0.0301} (\code{wasserstein}) and \code{1.3693 ± 0.0303} (\code{conformal}). Here and in the figure, ± denotes the standard error of the mean across ten seeds; lower error is better.
    }

    \p{
      Holm-adjusted two-sided sign-flip tests against \code{origin_only} give #{p \ge 0.738} at round 10,000; all four surrogate-free sharing configurations follow identical trajectories in this run.
    }

    \scope{
      \put\transclude/toc{false}
      \put\transclude/numbered{false}
      \put\transclude/heading{false}
      \transclude{baux-0010}
    }
  }
  }

  \subtree{
    \title{TORAX: A Transport Objective}

    \p{
      TORAX evolves radial plasma transport profiles (Citrin et al. [[Cit+24]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06718)). baux includes an evaluator that varies heating power and deposition location, runs the solver, and returns a negative loss based on central electron temperature, ion temperature and heating power. This gives the optimizer a concrete simulation objective with two bounded inputs.
    }

  }

  \subtree{
    \title{Device Geometry and Equilibria}

    \p{
      The console displays vessel and magnet geometry for TCV, SPARC, ARC, ITER and DIII-D. The non-TCV views include exported equilibrium and engineering data from FUSE (Meneghini et al. [[Men+24]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05894)).
    }
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Synthetic Objectives}

  \p{
    Choose from 56 test functions in the explorer below to inspect their domains and surfaces. To use the same catalogue in your own search, load a function through \code{baux.benchmarks.synthetic}.
  }

  \<div>[id]{ocaml-bench-simulation}{}

  \p{
    From Python, \code{baux.benchmarks.synthetic} returns bounds and an objective callable. The following example evaluates Ackley in 16 dimensions, then checks the evaluator at the origin:
  }

  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux
from baux.benchmarks import synthetic

bounds, objective = synthetic.problem("ackley", dim=16, workers=4)
state = baux.init(bounds, baux.enn(k=10) | baux.ucb(beta=2.0), key=baux.key(0))
for _ in range(12):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=8)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, objective(batch.points))

grid = np.zeros((4096, 16), dtype=np.float64)
vals = synthetic.eval_batch("ackley", grid, backend="simd", workers=4)
assert vals.shape == (4096,) and np.allclose(vals, 0.0, atol=1e-12)
print(f"4096 evals verified; TuRBO-ENN best_y={state.best_y:.5f}")
  }

  \p{
    Pass \code{backend="scalar"} or \code{backend="simd"} with \code{workers} to configure native parallel evaluation; see the [benchmarking note](baux-0011.html) for prepared-array versus end-to-end timings.
  }
}
